1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,724
Here is the US unemployment rate. Three people

2
00:00:02,824 --> 00:00:04,628
ask what it will do next month.

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,777
The economist wants its expected value. The risk manager wants to know how

4
00:00:09,877 --> 00:00:14,327
large a jump is possible. The trader wants the probability that it rises

5
00:00:14,427 --> 00:00:18,056
at all. Three different answers, and none of them is wrong.

6
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:21,643
They are looking at one object through different

7
00:00:21,743 --> 00:00:24,362
lenses: the distribution of tomorrow, given today.

8
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:27,908
Let us make that object visible. At each date,

9
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:30,763
the distribution of the next value, given the past,

10
00:00:30,863 --> 00:00:33,170
is a density riding just ahead of the path.

11
00:00:33,266 --> 00:00:37,833
Its centre, the conditional mean, hardly moves. Its five percent

12
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:42,500
quantile, in red, plunges every time the series turns turbulent,

13
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,802
and recovers when it calms. Same mean, very different risk.

14
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:51,296
The forecast is not a number, it is a whole distribution,

15
00:00:51,396 --> 00:00:54,117
and each target is a lens on it. Slide a guess

16
00:00:54,217 --> 00:00:57,491
m along the axis and record the expected squared error.

17
00:00:57,566 --> 00:01:00,031
The lowest point sits exactly at the mean.

18
00:01:00,131 --> 00:01:02,413
That is why the conditional mean is the

19
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:05,956
forecast that minimises the mean squared prediction error.

20
00:01:06,633 --> 00:01:08,824
Change the loss to the absolute error, and

21
00:01:08,924 --> 00:01:10,843
the lowest point moves to the median.

22
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:16,640
Make over-prediction nineteen times as costly as under-prediction, the

23
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:19,330
check loss, and the lowest point slides down to

24
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:21,906
the five percent quantile: the value at risk.

25
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:27,126
And the variance measures the spread. Each object is the best forecast

26
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:31,355
under its own loss. Choosing the object means choosing the loss.

27
00:01:32,433 --> 00:01:35,601
Now the second question of the lecture: how many

28
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:39,006
lags? Every information criterion is a tug of war.

29
00:01:39,106 --> 00:01:42,547
Fit pulls towards more lags, the penalty pulls back.

30
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:46,667
Suppose the extra lag is useless. Its gain in fit

31
00:01:46,767 --> 00:01:50,312
is still random: a chi-square with one degree of freedom.

32
00:01:50,412 --> 00:01:52,806
That is the strength of the green team.

33
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,128
Akaike charges a price of two per lag. The useless lag

34
00:01:56,228 --> 00:02:00,010
wins the tug whenever its gain exceeds two, which happens about

35
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:03,092
sixteen percent of the time, at every sample size.

36
00:02:04,166 --> 00:02:08,982
Schwarz charges log T. As the sample grows, his rope gets thicker, the

37
00:02:09,082 --> 00:02:13,828
red line moves right, and the chance of keeping a useless lag shrinks

38
00:02:13,928 --> 00:02:18,042
towards zero. That is why BIC is consistent, and AIC is not.

39
00:02:19,133 --> 00:02:23,163
So: tomorrow is a distribution, and the loss you care about decides

40
00:02:23,263 --> 00:02:26,739
which feature of it to forecast. And the number of lags is

41
00:02:26,839 --> 00:02:29,883
settled by a tug of war, in which only a price that

42
00:02:29,983 --> 00:02:32,904
grows with the sample keeps the useless ones out.

